contentText:
Harry Styles gave a cheeky nod to ex-girlfriend Taylor Swift&#8217;s wedding during night three of his Madison Square Garden residency on Saturday (Aug. 29).

Toward the end of the set, he hyped up the venue&#8217;s recent history before pivoting to the joke: &#8220;We&#8217;re at Madison Square Garden, home of the world champion New York Knicks!&#8221; he told the crowd, before adding, &#8220;And they also do weddings.&#8221; The line, referencing Taylor Swift and Travis Kelce &#8216;s July 3 wedding at the arena, drew loud cheers.

Styles and Swift dated from November 2012 to January 2013, a relationship widely understood to have inspired Swift&#8217;s 2014 hit &#8220;Style.&#8221; He reportedly received an invitation to the Swift-Kelce wedding but didn&#8217;t attend; sources told Variety at the time he was &#8220;currently on tour and will not make it to MSG.&#8221;

His fiancée, Zoë Kravitz, went to the ceremony without him. Styles was performing the London leg of his own Together, Together tour that same night, and fan footage from the July 3 Wembley show captured him singing 2017 track &#8220;Two Ghosts&#8221; — long rumored to be his response to &#8220;Style&#8221; — including the lines &#8220;Same lips red, same eyes blue / Same white shirt, couple more tattoos.&#8221;

The joke landed three days into a residency already drawing outsized attention. Styles&#8217; 30-show MSG run — which makes up the entirety of his 2026 U.S. tour dates and continues through Halloween — opened Aug. 26 to 11.5 million presale ticket registrations in January, which Ticketmaster called the highest volume ever recorded for an artist presale.

It&#8217;s Styles&#8217; first run of live shows since 2023, in support of his new album Kiss All the Time. Disco, Occasionally , released in March.

Opening night carried its own emotional weight. Styles completely overhauled his usual setlist — a departure from the largely unchanged show he&#8217;d played across 33 prior tour dates — and paused late in the set to honor Dolly Parton, who had died the day before.

&#8220;I just wanna give a proper thank you to all the musicians who have inspired me over the years,&#8221; he told the crowd. &#8220;Dolly, we love you. Thank you so much.&#8221; Her 1974 recording of &#8220;I Will Always Love You&#8221; then played over the PA as the crowd sang along, with Zoë Kravitz, Gayle King and Drew Barrymore among those in attendance. Styles closed that show with &#8220;Sign of the Times&#8221; and set-closer &#8220;Aperture.&#8221;
